WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:11.190
The conundrum for U.S. policymakers in the
fight against ISIS is what to do about Iran

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.690
and Iran's efforts to combat the terrorist
group themselves.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.890
On the one hand, it looks like that we would
have similar interests to Iran.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:26.050
They both oppose the radical Sunni extremist
group and they also are looking to keep Iraq

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.470
as a single country.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.730
On those two points, we agree.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.369
ISIS should be defeated, Iraq should stay
together.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:38.790
But at the same time, they have a different
vision where Iraq, Syria, Lebanon, other countries

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.950
are coming more under the Iranian sphere.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.690
The challenge for this White House, which
it's not willing to take right now, and maybe

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:51.490
a President Clinton or President Trump would,
is to have a more confrontational policy towards

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:56.080
Iran in its activities in Iraq and Syria and
Lebanon.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.990
We're very clear where we're not going to
allow these militia groups and other proxies

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:06.970
of Iran to become the dominant player in Iraq
or Syria's or Lebanon's security and political

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.530
structures.

